From 0ff164414e8dfacbc92f4b99c031d1e05b909744 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raiju contributors <raiju@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:55:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: node:fs renameSync, rename, openSync, writeSync,
 fsyncSync, fdatasyncSync, closeSync and chmodSync; writeFileSync's mode and
 flush; Stats.mode

node:fs could write a file only in place (writeFileSync truncates, then writes) and had no
rename or fsync, so a program could not replace a file atomically: one that dies mid-write
leaves it truncated, and a reader at that moment sees the same. The usual cure, a temporary
file fsynced and renamed over the target and the directory fsynced, needs these calls.

renameSync and rename (the callback form, run as a microtask) are POSIX rename. openSync takes
Node's flag strings (r, r+, w, wx, w+, wx+, a, ax, a+, ax+; "r" opens a directory too) and a
mode, with O_CLOEXEC; an unknown string is EINVAL. writeSync writes all of a string or bytes,
retrying short writes and EINTR. fsyncSync and fdatasyncSync use F_FULLFSYNC on macOS. chmodSync
sets a mode. writeFileSync takes { mode, flush }: with either it goes through open, write,
fsync and close; without, it keeps its old path. Stats gains mode (st_mode). Errors carry Node's
messages, with both paths for rename ("ENOENT: no such file or directory, rename 'a' -> 'b'")
and the call for descriptors ("EBADF: bad file descriptor, fsync"); the error table learns
EBADF, EXDEV and EINVAL.

Tests: tests/run/native_fs_atomic.tov (17 checks): rename over an existing file, its errors, a
file onto a directory, open/write/fsync/close and the created mode, wx and a, unknown flags, a
directory fsynced, a closed descriptor, writeFileSync's mode and flush, chmodSync, and the
callback form with and without an error.
